Doctoral researcher (Ph.D.) position at UTN with the topic of Mathematical Optimization for Multi-Robot Systems (m/f/d)

The Department of Liberal Arts & Social Sciences is currently offering one opening for a doctoral research opportunity (fully funded, 3 years, 100%, pay scale TV-L E 13) in the Analytics and Optimization Group led by Prof. Dr. Alexander Martin. We are seeking a highly motivated and talented individual to join our dynamic and international research team.

The successful candidate will join an interdisciplinary team of researchers developing novel approaches to multi-agent path finding in large-scale robotic warehouses. The position is rooted in mathematical optimization and involves close collaboration with researchers in robotics and business intelligence. You will conduct research on topics such as mixed-integer optimization for multi-agent path finding, optimization under uncertainty, and optimization-based conflict resolution, and contribute mathematical optimization expertise to the interdisciplinary team. The developed methods will be evaluated in simulation and on real robotic systems.

There will be a complementary position rooted in .

Further involved research groups are the Multi-Robot Systems Junior Research Group led by Dr. Tanja Kaiser, the Discrete Optimization Group led by Prof. Dr. Johannes Thürauf, and FAU’s Management Intelligence Services Group led by Prof. Dr. Freimut Bodendorf.

**Your tasks:**
---------------

The successful candidate will contribute to the interdisciplinary project “Real-World Multi-Agent Optimal Path Finding under Uncertainty”.

Your responsibilities will include:

* Conducting cutting-edge research on mathematical optimization for multi-agent path finding in close collaboration with an interdisciplinary team.
* Developing mathematical optimization methods, such as mixed-integer optimization approaches, for multi-agent path planning and complex conflict resolution in real-world robotic systems.
* Developing optimization approaches under uncertainty to obtain reliable solutions under uncertain robot behavior, demand, and operating conditions.
* Implementing and computationally evaluating the developed optimization methods, including their evaluation in real-world robotic systems.
* Closely collaborating with researchers in robotics and FAU’s Management Intelligence Services Group to integrate optimization, robotics, and business intelligence.
* Publishing research results in leading international journals and conferences and presenting your work to the global research community at international conferences.
* Contributing to teaching activities and student supervision (e.g., courses, theses, research projects).
* Engaging in outreach activities and communicating research to academic, industrial, and wider public audiences.

**Your profile:**
-----------------

We expect:

* A qualifying degree (M.Sc. or equivalent) in Mathematics, Computer Science, Operations Research, or a related discipline including natural sciences and engineering
* Motivation, dedication, and a strong sense of responsibility
* A solid mathematical background and strong interest in mathematical optimization
* Strong interest in interdisciplinary research
* Programming experience, e.g., Python, C++, or related languages
* Proficiency in English (spoken and written)

Additional experience in one or more of the following areas is desirable:

* Mixed-integer optimization and mathematical programming
* Optimization under uncertainty
* Optimization software (e.g., Gurobi, CPLEX, SCIP)
* Algorithm development and computational optimization

Selected highlights of this position include:

* A unique opportunity to conduct research at the intersection of mathematical optimization, robotics, and business intelligence, while developing deep expertise in optimization and collaborating closely across disciplines.
* The opportunity to develop novel mathematical optimization methods and evaluate them on challenging real-world multi-robot systems.
* Access to broad expertise across UTN’s groups and departments, as well as computational resources.
